  "response": "# Review: 03-node-cli-tool (Commander.js added on an empty baseline), 51 findings\n\n**Verdict: nothing here should block the merge.** The scanner's highest `agent_risk` is 0.46, and I'd only block at about 0.5 or above with confirmed evidence. I opened the files behind the riskiest findings. The two biggest groups of \"high-risk\" findings, plus the hardcoded-localhost finding, are false positives. What's left is a short list of warnings.\n\n## Block merge: none\n\n## Warn (fix in this PR or open a follow-up ticket)\n\n| Finding | Severity | agent_risk | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| crime_00001 `\"PORT\"` repeated across 5 files | medium | **0.46** (highest) | It's real, but 4 of the 5 are separate standalone demos in `examples/`, plus one type test. Pulling it into a shared constant would make the examples worse as standalone samples. Warn only: low blast radius (0). The 0.75 test_gap is the only reason the score is high. |\n| crime_00002 `myParseInt` duplicated in 2 examples | medium | 0.33 | Same situation: duplication in demo files is intentional so each example is self-contained. Warn without asking for a change. |\n| crime_00013 / crime_00020 `choices()` duplicated in `lib/argument.js:99` and `lib/option.js:176` | medium | 0.18 | This is the duplication that matters, because it's production library code. A fix to the choices validation could land in one class and not the other. 00020 is the inner part of 00013, so it's one issue counted twice. Worth extracting into a shared helper. |\n| crime_00006 / crime_00007 broken doc links in `Readme_zh-CN.md` and `docs/zh-CN/` | low | 0.24 | Confidence is high (0.9): the linked zh-CN pages (glossary, variadic options, deprecated features) really aren't in the PR. Either add the files or drop the links. |\n| crime_00019 / 00024 / 00025 / 00033 / 00036: god files (`typings/index.d.ts` 891 lines, `lib/command.js` 2179, `typings/index.test-d.ts`, `lib/help.js`, `lib/option.js`) | 00019 and 00024 high, the other three medium | 0.06–0.15 | Severity is high, but agent_risk is low: churn is 0 and blast radius is close to 0. `lib/command.js` at 7.3× the threshold deserves a refactor ticket. It shouldn't gate this merge. The `.d.ts` file is a single public API surface and can reasonably stay in one file. |\n| crime_00028 / 00034 / 00043 / 00051: long methods (`_executeSubCommand` 120 lines, `parseOptions` 115, `_parseCommand` 80, `addOption` 69) | 00028 high, the others medium | 0.07–0.09 | The same follow-up ticket as `command.js`. `_executeSubCommand` (high severity) is the first one to break up. |\n\n## Ignore\n\n**Confirmed false positives (I checked the source):**\n\n- **crime_00003, 00008, 00015, 00021, 00026, 00031, 00038, 00044** (`tests/ts-imports.test.ts`, \"no assertions\"; medium severity, agent_risk 0.31). Each test calls `checkClass()`, and that helper runs two `expect` calls (lines 10–11). The detector doesn't follow assertions into helper functions. These eight are the reason the weak-test category looks risky, and none of them is real.\n- **crime_00004, 00011, 00018, 00023, 00029, 00035, 00042, 00049** (`tests/command.exitOverride.test.js`, \"no assertions\"; medium, 0.27). Each test ends with `expectCommanderError(...)`, which makes four `expect` calls. The file even registers it with the linter (`jest/expect-expect` `assertFunctionNames`, line 9). Same helper blind spot.\n- **crime_00012**, `localhost:1234` in `lib/command.js:2152` (medium, 0.2). It's an `// e.g. --inspect=localhost:1234` comment next to the regex that parses Node `--inspect` flags. No URL is hardcoded.\n\n**Real but harmless:**\n\n- **crime_00009, 00010, 00016, 00017, 00022, 00027, 00032, 00039, 00045**, \"weak matchers\" (low, 0.23). The tests use `toBeTruthy()` / `toBeFalsy()` on boolean flags such as `_hasHelpOption` and `.variadic`, after checking the state before the change. Tightening them to `toBe(true)` / `toBe(false)` is a nice-to-have.\n- **crime_00005**, \"logic in comments\" (low, 0.27). It's a historical note about SIGUSR1 printing a debugger message. No rule is hidden in it, and the \"retry\" match comes from the quoted error message.\n- **crime_00014**, \"commented-out code\" (low, 0.18). These lines document which `spawn` signatures the mock checks for on each platform. They're explanation, not dead code.\n- **crime_00030, 00037, 00040, 00041, 00046**, long test `describe` blocks (low/medium, 0.04–0.08). Long test suites are normal.\n- **crime_00047, 00048, 00050**, `CHANGELOG.md` and the READMEs over 1,000 lines (low, 0.03–0.04). Splitting a changelog or README isn't worth it.\n\n## Summary\n\n- **Block:** none.\n- **Warn:** 15 findings. The ones that matter most are the duplicated `choices()` in `lib/` (00013 and 00020, one issue counted twice), the broken zh-CN doc links, and `lib/command.js` with its four long methods as a refactor follow-up.\n- **Ignore:** 36 findings. Seventeen of them are false positives: the 16 \"no assertions\" tests, where the detector misses assertions inside helpers, plus the localhost comment.\n\n**Feedback for the scanner:** `weak_test_signal/no_assertions` should follow local helper functions that call `expect`, and should respect `jest/expect-expect` `assertFunctionNames`. `hardcoded_localhost` should skip comments. Those two changes would remove 17 of the 51 findings, including the 8 test findings scored at 0.31.",
